# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INRP2P sandbox — deployment configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local for local work. For a deployment, set these in the
# hosting provider's environment settings. The first three are REQUIRED in
# production: the app fails closed on each rather than degrading quietly.
# The Telegram pair is optional — without it the web app is unaffected and
# only the Mini App is unavailable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 1. PostgreSQL. Any hosted provider works (Neon, Supabase, Vercel Postgres).
#    Locally, `npm run db:start` runs an embedded server and you can leave
#    this unset.
#    Apply the schema once with:  npm run db:migrate
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=require

# 2. Acknowledges that this deployment serves the funds-free sandbox.
#    Without it, production refuses to construct the escrow adapter, because
#    an unlabelled simulation in front of real users is the failure mode that
#    guard exists to prevent.
INRP2P_SANDBOX=true

# 3. Session cookie signing key. MUST be a long random value — the
#    development fallback is committed to this public repository, so anyone
#    could forge a session with it, including an operator one.
#      openssl rand -base64 32
SANDBOX_SESSION_SECRET=replace-me-with-32-random-bytes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram Mini App (optional)
#
# Leave both unset and nothing changes: the web app works exactly as before,
# and the Mini App simply refuses to sign anyone in.
#
# Setting it up, once, in @BotFather:
#   /newbot                  → create the bot, copy the token below
#   /newapp                  → attach a Mini App, give it your HTTPS URL
#                              and a short name (the `/app` in the link)
#   /setmenubutton           → optional: open the app from the chat header
#
# The deployment MUST be served over HTTPS. Telegram will not accept an
# http:// URL, and the session cookie the Mini App receives is `Secure`, so
# a plain-HTTP host would drop it and the sign-in would appear to succeed
# and then not stick.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 4. SECRET. The bot token from @BotFather.
#    This is the key every Mini App launch signature is verified against —
#    anyone holding it can forge a launch for any Telegram user, which means
#    signing in as them. Treat it exactly like a private key: never commit
#    it, never expose it to the browser, rotate it with /revoke if it leaks.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:AA-replace-me-with-the-token-from-botfather

# 5. PUBLIC. The Mini App's address, used to build share links that open
#    inside Telegram rather than in a browser. Not a secret — it is the
#    address you want people to send each other.
#    Format: https://t.me/<bot-username>/<app-short-name>
NEXT_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_MINI_APP=https://t.me/YourBotUsername/app
